WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.760
Hey everyone, Jared here.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.240
Today, we’re talking about a show that’s at the top of its game after almost 20 years on the air:

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:23.700
 South Park, specifically, season 19.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:30.620
Unlike much earlier seasons, Season 19 is a serialized narrative that tells an overarching story from episode to episode.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:35.200
They played with this idea a bit in Season 18, but, overall, it wasn’t nearly as comprehensive. 

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.340
Season 19 is arguably the smartest season yet.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.540
It chronicles the small Colorado town as it experiences significant social changes,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:49.580
introduces a new central character, and comments on America’s current political climate.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:56.100
One of the cleverest aspects of this season
is its layered commentary.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.580
Not only is it about our modern social condition, but also
about the show itself.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:06.420
The season opens with the firing of Principal Victoria over an incident referring to rape as a “hot cosby”.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.400
"A new person has been appointed to try and make South Park Elementary a more progressive place

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.300
that fits in with today's times."

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.840
He might as well be talking about South Park the show.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.500
"I'm here because this place is lost in a time-warp."

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.840
In other words, can South Park’s signature vulgar sensibility possibly survive

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.200
in our current politically correct world?

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.560
You could go through the season and interpret these comments on the town changing

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.600
as a meta commentary on the show itself facing change.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.140
"The times changing so fast. Everybody wanna be PC, be updated. It's like I'm a relic."

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.020
The Mayor later
makes this explicit to Officer Barbrady,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.220
"You're from another time, Barbrady. And the last thing that needs to go."

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.200
the Newsmen are constantly droning on about noticing “changes” to the town, and Mr. Garrison —

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:57.920
himself a fountain of unbridled profanity
— returns to South Park to save it from these changes.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.940
But what’s the deal with the PC plotline? One might be satisfied with the explanation that

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.320
 it’s Trey Parker and Matt Stone taking a dump on liberal politics as they've done in the past.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.520
The two have espoused their libertarian
leanings in interviews before,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.120
but they often evade a simple left/right divide.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.280
If you felt slightly lost in this connection between the ads, PC culture, and gentrification, you’re not alone. 

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:25.820
But find solace, because we've got an idea, and it all starts with neoliberalism.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:33.380
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The Philosophy of South Park Season 19.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.540
In a nutshell, neoliberalism is an economic
ideology

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.740
that stresses personal freedoms and market solutions to address society’s problems.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.900
Want to fix healthcare, the post
office or Chipotle? Let the market take care of it.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.720
Or as Philosopher Henry Giroux puts it, the
tenants of neoliberalism are:

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.860
1. Consumerism is the most important duty as a citizen, 2. Individual freedoms trump public freedoms

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.640
and 3. All public social issues are addressed on a private level.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.920
Neoliberalism neatly ties together three seemingly
disparate themes in the show:

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.760
gentrification, PC culture and advertising.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.200
Which leads us to one of the best lines of the season:

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.900
"What is PC but a verbal form of gentrification?

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:16.720
"Spruce everything up, get rid of all the ugliness, in order to create a false sense of paradise?

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.480
"Only one thing can actually live in that world: ads."

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.780
Neoliberalism has one goal: make everything
marketable.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:30.920
Gentrification, for example, is the free market’s way of dealing with shitty neighborhoods.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.980
 It’s the process whereby real estate developers and business interests

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:38.700
take advantage of low prices and industrial chic to sell white people overpriced tapas

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.340
without fixing the underlying problem of poverty.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.040
Even diversity and multiculturalism serve
the engines of profit.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.240
Wanna venture to Shi Tpa Town to experience exotic cuisines of other cultures?

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.780
Well, don’t feel too dignified in your worldliness.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:58.140
By suggesting that advertisers are manipulating PC culture in order to gentrify run-down areas all over the world, 

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.540
the show playfully points to something they've been saying for years; that in a neoliberal economy,

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:08.380
otherness and diversity have just become marketable brands in order to sell anything and everything.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.320
This is all made pretty explicit in the show:

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.060
"Oh great. So now ads are using PC for their own games? That's a new low."

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:20.140
One might say that the ads are a stand-in
for the grossly inhuman forces of neoliberal capitalism,

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:24.120
"And now, the ads have taken the next step in their evolution. They have taken human form."

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.200
ready to assimilate or destroy everything and everyone in its path — 

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.720
but always behind the scenes, and barely noticeable. Leslie and PC Principal stirs up an anxiety:

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:39.140
what if my friend, family, or beloved TV shows are just ads or sponsored content?

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.160
What if I’m an ad?

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.720
In a world permeated by advertising, how can we know what ideas are truly our own, 

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.720
rather than planted by clever commercials?

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.960
What makes the ads so dangerous is their ability to cover up the reality of the situation, to distract us.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.740
In the end, as Nathan says, it’s only ads
that can live in this world.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:02.160
Reduced versions of ourselves, of cultures, that can be neatly packaged and sold.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.180
Cultures of social acceptance that are more of a brand than actual tolerance.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.840
Gay pride parades sponsored by TD Bank, or real standards of beauty sponsored by Dove,

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:16.060
because who needs real revolution when there’s a clever hashtag?

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.240
Now now, we’re not arguing that diversity is bad,
and neither is South Park. 

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.360
But one thing neoliberalism is really good at is a process called cooptation —

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.660
to take what was once outside of the system and assimilate it so that it can serve the needs of capitalism.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:36.180
Neoliberalism absorbs identities and diversity, so that multiculturalism becomes a trendy brand to be purchased.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.540
"Is there a reason the children with disabilities and black child are front and center?"

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.800
"Oh no, that's just where they always sit. Next to our beloved Craig there, who is a homosexual."

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.300
For the town of South Park to advance economically,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.480
they have to adopt these marks of tolerance to bring in businesses and new residents. 

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.620
So how do you convince the world that your town isn’t some antiquated backwater shithole, 

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.020
but rather a bastion of progressive ideals?

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.060
Whole Foods. The show uses Whole Foods as a focal point for a ton of the progressive changes happening.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:08.740
"Our town has only had a Whole Foods for three weeks, and we already have our first gay kids. So cool."

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.580
When the whole town think Craig and Tweek are gay,

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:14.780
Whole Foods serves as the setting where everyone congratulates Craig’s dad on the news.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.820
Randy even credits Whole Foods for their new police-free environment:

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:22.820
"We've only had a Whole Foods for a month, and already we don't need cops. So cool."

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.180
Whole Foods has ceased to be a place where we can buy organic food

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:33.139
and instead becomes a set of lofty ideals that we buy into every time we purchase that $6 bottle of Asparagus Water.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.700
That kind of branding is good for not only Whole Foods, but for television.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.500
Here the metacommentary gets pretty explicit again.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.500
People shower Craig and Tweak with money for being gay.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.080
The message is clear: the spectacle of inclusion is good for business.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.700
The episode ends with a crowd of voyeurs watching Craig and Tweek through a window,

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.760
 raising the question of whether the affirmation is for them or for the viewer.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.720
In the new neoliberal economy, the spectacle of inclusion is sold just like any other commodity. 

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.160
We fork over some cash and, in return, we get a healthy dose of self righteousness.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.500
Slovenian theorist and curmudgeonly mountain-troll Slavoj Zizek

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:11.280
thinks that modern political systems can be much more dangerous than they were in the days of yore

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.939
because they’re just so dang pleasant.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:18.400
In the face of dictators, bad bosses, or the
KKK, it’s easy to point a finger and say

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.920
“Hey, that’s the bad guy, fuck him.” In
other words, this —

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.620
"And then after we've f*cked every last one of them to death, we build a big wall."

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.380
 is easier to hate than this —

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.980
"Hello ladies, thanks for coming to our event!"
"Hi!"

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:33.380
"We're just tired of the way people with disabilities get treated. Can I get you some alcohol?"

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.380
Being a PC bro is still about getting laid
and wasted,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:42.360
but now they have a pleasant facade to hide their real motivations.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.660
Zizek describes political correctness as 

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:50.460
“just a form of self-discipline which doesn’t really allow you to overcome [racism, misogyny, and so on.]"

00:07:50.460 --> 00:08:00.460
If PC culture is dangerous, it’s because it’s just a nice paint job over the same old discriminatory reality.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:06.240
There’s no longer a clear villain to rally against, just self-satisfying acts of social justice.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.540
The new “progressive” world of South Park is one where people regularly blow hundreds of dollars at Whole Foods,

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:15.320
but are expected to feel good because they donated $1 to charity.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.660
Never mind the wasteful excess of your grocery
list, 

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:21.100
you can wash yourself of the guilt with a token gesture of charity.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.620
"$26 in fillet mignons, and $1 for hungry kids around the world.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:30.140
On the one hand, we have those who criticize neoliberalism but don't actually do anything about it.

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:32.680
On the other, we have those who actively oppose it.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:38.400
Zizek argues that this kind of Whole Food style charity is the perfect marriage between these two camps —

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.360
between inaction and action.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:45.140
You don’t actually have to change the world — change and rebellion become another commodity.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.180
 Criticism is perfectly disarmed,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:51.779
because you can be a good neoliberal consumer,
and still feel like you’re fighting against it.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:55.720
Zizek argues that rebelling against the system is tolerated, even promoted,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.380
so long as it stays within the acceptable bounds.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.180
He uses the term inter-passivity,

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.380
which denotes the practice of doing things NOT in order to achieve something.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:10.420
"Political correctness,” he says, "fits the formula of ‘Let’s go on changing something all the time so that, globally,

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:11.780
things will remain the same.’”

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:18.480
Of course, South Park attacks the very heart of this hypocrisy.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:27.600
The town’s real feelings about the poor are brought to light when Whole Foods gets surrounded by the homeless.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.640
It’s not just economic hypocrisy that South Park targets.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.200
 Multiculturalism becomes a kind of badge to wear, to buy into, rather than a real political project.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.460
"What do you want?"

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.440
"Now look, not all cops are racist trigger-happy assholes."

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:43.700
"Really? I'll bet you don't even know what 'farm to table' means."

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.420
The denizens of South Park are still just as ignorant,

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.920
but now know the benefits of feigning knowledge of other cultures.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.740
"You know, a lot of people think that Asians decide who's gay and who's not.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.900
"But it isn't all Asians. It's just specifically the Japanese, OK?"

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.480
"You sure know a lot about this."
"Yes, I do."

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.840
The show is criticizing the individual just as much as they’re calling out other shows.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:10.240
The metacriticism raises the question: does token inclusion really make for more inclusivity,

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:17.160
or is it a shell of actual diversity that is meant to boost ratings rather than understand another culture?

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:21.010
In the end, this show of tolerance is more
about making white liberals feel good than

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.360
the marginalized people they seek to help.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.560
"You know, there's still some people out there that say, 'What does being PC really mean?'

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.840
"Well I'll tell you what it means. It means you love nothing more than beer, working out,

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:35.240
and that feeling you get when you rhetorically defend a marginalized community from systems of oppression."

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:41.100
The PC frathouse, for all the talk about marginalized
people of color, is, well, entirely white.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:45.120
Even their fervent passion to help the disabled
is just a cover to crush puss.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:50.280
French philosopher Jean Baudrillard uses the term charity cannibalism to denote the process whereby

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.840
“Other people's destitution becomes
our adventure playground...

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:59.220
[where we continually seek to...] secure the conditions of reproduction of the catastrophe market.”

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:03.740
 According to Baudrillard, when we run out of tragedies to feel bad about, we start inventing our own.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:08.120
"Why would a person with a disability not see that what I I'm trying to do is actually protect them?"

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.520
"Sometimes, victims of oppression are so marginalized that they begin to sympathize with their oppressors."

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:13.160
"Yeah, brah!"

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:18.000
When PC Principle encounters a marginalized
person, Jimmy, who doesn’t want his help,

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.440
he finds Nathan who more aptly suits his narrative of the world.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:26.380
 Nathan plays the role of a helpless victim whose feelings of outrage reflect PC Principal’s.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:30.260
"Could you tell Jimmy how you feel when people use the word 'retarded'?"

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:35.360
"It hurts my feelings because I feel bad."
"... Are you serious right now?"

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:40.140
The point is not that using the word “retarded” is OK because someone with a disability uses it,

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:44.500
it’s that this relation of protector-victim is always disempowering to the victim,

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.600
 who will only be validated by virtue of their helplessness.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.860
This is why, Zizek would argue, this sort
of PC culture is more intolerant to other cultures:

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:58.660
we lovingly accept everyone, so long as we can pigeonhole them into a type of difference we can consume.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:02.200
Nevermind that Caitlyn Jenner, who is of course a stunning and brave hero,

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:11.300
was accused of killing a person and is critical of gay marriage,

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:16.200
it’s much easier to congratulate ourselves as a society by plastering her on Vanity Fair. 

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:21.660
It’s simple, it’s consumable, it sells.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.760
I should probably clarify here that people throw around the term “political correctness” and “safe space”

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:31.040
in an entirely vague and not very helpful way. On the one end, you have some guy 

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:39.760
 for whom every social interaction resembles a 12-year-old who just learned the N-Word on X-Box Live.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.580
On the other end, you have people who confuse social justice for wanting to live in a bubble 

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:49.120
where nothing bad happens and terrible things like slavery never existed.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:53.640
These people on both sides are few and far between, so please stop freaking out.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:57.080
South Park reliably takes down both sides of the political extreme,

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:08.080
making fun of both Trump-esque onservativism and, well, everyone who got a liberal arts degree.

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:13.019
What the show is really saying is that behind both sides, there lies a deep rooted narcissism.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.540
As we’ve already discussed, 

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:19.740
a lot of buying into the PC brand is more about making yourself feel good than affecting real change. 

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.620
The Whole Foods donation arc ends with Randy morphing a charity about feeding hungry kids,

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.960
or sending hamsters to college, or whatever,

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:32.840
into a charity where kids in the developing world are given iPads to protect the fragile egos of people like Randy.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:34.240
"For just $1 a day, 

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:39.020
"one of these beautiful children will protect you from trolls and make your internet a safe space."

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:41.380
And that’s what this Whole Foods activism is about:

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:46.500
being a sell-out to the system while making meaningless gestures to protect your own sense of self-worth.

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:52.320
Then there’s Cartman, who we’ve known for years is a terrible monster, yet suddenly adopts PC culture.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.540
But we also know he’s a pathological liar and raging narcissist, 

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:59.640
so it makes sense that he’d use safe space
language to protect his ego.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:03.780
On the other side of things, we have Mr. Garrison and his fuck-em-all-to-death attitude.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:10.060
His version of American exceptionalism is more or less the nationalist version of the “special snowflake” complex.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:13.370
 Convinced that America’s unique greatness is being ruined by Canadians,

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.400
he tries to build a wall to protect America.

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.840
What is his shock to the Canadian wall being put up first to keep HIM out

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:23.360
but the realization that he’s not the center of the universe?

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.220
In other words, 

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:32.060
Cartman, Steven Seagal and Garrison supporters are fueled by a narcissistic obsession with themselves.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:34.320
Here’s where neoliberalism enters the picture again.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:38.420
Neoliberalism promotes a culture of consumerism centered on the individual.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:46.440
And nothing quite fuels that economy like self obsession en masse. What’s left is a cult of victimhood.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:54.300
For Mr. Garrison, you have the paranoid delusion that everyone’s out to ruin your way of life and steal your job.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.680
For the show’s PC crowd,  it’s the mistaken idea that not being a victim of harassment

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:01.340
means having someone entertain your’s illusions of grandeur.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:10.020
For those already privileged, it means aspiring to victimhood. In the end, everyone wants to hang reality.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:13.020
The point is not that everyone advocating for safe spaces, 

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.760
or everyone who loves their country, is one of these extreme caricatures, 

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:22.660
but that otherwise reasonable logic has been caught up in a whirlwind of societal bullshit.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:26.700
The creators of South Park probably don’t hate every aspect of PC culture,

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:33.440
but the shallow versions of it propagated in the mainstream media.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.020
 After all, PC Principal has a sort of redemption: 

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:42.120
after learning he’s been manipulated by ads, he goes on a rampage murdering all the “inauthentic” PC bros. 

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:46.520
Creators Matt Stone and Trey Parker are also acutely aware of their own privileged position,

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:48.020
 nodding to this in the show:

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:52.000
"We're two privileged straight white boys who have theirs laughs about things we've never had to deal with."

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.760
And the finale makes it explicit that PC Principal is here to stay, with Stan commenting:

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:58.120
"This is gonna be really hard."

00:15:58.120 --> 00:15:59.600
Because, well, it is.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:10.060
Not wanting to seem like a bigot while avoiding the trappings of phony inclusion is really hard. 

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:12.320
With season 20 around the corner, what will happen?

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:18.740
Will the show yield to the pressures of a changing society and reorient itself completely? 

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:29.740
Or will they stick their middle finger firmly in the air and double down on their off-color roots?

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:33.440
South Park has to navigate our current political climate, whether they like it or not,

